WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.001 --> 00:00:04.421
The gloom of sadness and woe of a windless night settled over the men like a blanket.

2
00:00:04.422 --> 00:00:07.706
Odysseus urged his men to row, but their hearts weren't in it.

3
00:00:07.707 --> 00:00:12.555
The men thought they were forsaken, cursed by the gods, and despair had taken hold of each of them.

4
00:00:12.556 --> 00:00:15.968
All through the night Odysseus pleaded and begged for them to row.

5
00:00:15.969 --> 00:00:24.278
Finally as a finger of gray stirred in the east, Odysseus addressed his men in anger, "You believe we are cursed, so you refuse to row.

6
00:00:24.279 --> 00:00:27.659
But by the grace of some mighty god, we were spared destruction.

7
00:00:27.660 --> 00:00:31.215
Although some gods may want us dead, others want us to live.

8
00:00:31.216 --> 00:00:36.413
None of us know what is written on the endless scroll kept by fate. A new day is dawning.

9
00:00:36.414 --> 00:00:43.302
Our hearts are heavy, but let us fortify our minds and will our bodies forward. Drummer boy, give us a lively beat."

10
00:00:43.303 --> 00:00:50.374
As the boy beat a lively rhythm on the drums, Odysseus chanted a poem that brightened his men's hearts, as much as the sun brightened the day.

11
00:00:50.375 --> 00:00:53.263
By noon, they could see another island on the horizon.

12
00:00:53.264 --> 00:00:59.679
When they reached the shore, they drew lots; half the men would stay with the ship and the other half would explore the island.

13
00:00:59.680 --> 00:01:07.655
As fate would have it, Odysseus was to stay with the ship, and Eurylocus, the ship's lieutenant, was to lead twenty-two men in exploration of the island.

14
00:01:07.656 --> 00:01:13.409
Eurylocus and his men headed straight for the center of the island because they hoped to find a fresh water spring.

15
00:01:13.410 --> 00:01:16.230
Instead they found a cottage made of cut stone.

16
00:01:16.231 --> 00:01:22.400
The house was surrounded by an assortment of wild animals: lions, wolves, bears, and many other creatures.

17
00:01:22.401 --> 00:01:27.034
Eurylocus told the men to approach the animals with caution, but the animals were friendly.

18
00:01:27.035 --> 00:01:32.119
They did not attack the men, but wagged their tails and rubbed against the men like some tamed dogs or cats.

19
00:01:32.120 --> 00:01:38.809
"The creature that lives within," said Eurylocus to his men, "must have powerful enchantment to tame such wild beasts."

20
00:01:38.810 --> 00:01:41.423
"She is no enchantress," said one of the men.

21
00:01:41.424 --> 00:01:45.938
"She's beautiful." In the sunlit room, there was a beautiful woman at her loom.

22
00:01:45.939 --> 00:01:49.653
She was singing a merry song, and weaving cloth of dazzling beauty.

23
00:01:49.654 --> 00:01:53.740
Before Eurylocus could caution his men, one of them called out to the woman.

24
00:01:53.741 --> 00:01:55.788
She waved to them and invited them in.

25
00:01:55.789 --> 00:02:02.637
Eurylocus believed that danger often comes in beautiful packaging, and he refused to go into the house with the rest of his men.

26
00:02:02.638 --> 00:02:06.566
"Welcome. I am Circe," said the beautiful woman to the men.

27
00:02:06.567 --> 00:02:10.927
"Please sit at my table, and have dinner with me, because I rarely have guests upon my island."

28
00:02:10.928 --> 00:02:13.561
The men accepted her invitation eagerly.

29
00:02:13.562 --> 00:02:19.308
The sorceress loaded her table with many good things to eat: cheese, fruit, wine, and bread.

30
00:02:19.309 --> 00:02:23.689
She watched them eat the food. The men did not know that the food was enchanted.

31
00:02:23.690 --> 00:02:26.734
Soon they forgot about their homes, and even their names.

32
00:02:26.735 --> 00:02:30.774
Then, with a flick of her wand, she turned them all into squealing pigs.

33
00:02:30.775 --> 00:02:35.759
Eurylocus, who was watching from outside, was horrified, and hurried back to Odysseus.

34
00:02:35.760 --> 00:02:40.252
When he reached Odysseus and the rest of the men, he was so dismayed that he could not speak.

35
00:02:40.253 --> 00:02:45.371
Odysseus didn't need to hear words to know that something terrible had happened to his comrades.

36
00:02:45.372 --> 00:02:49.236
"You needn't speak," said Odysseus. "Just show me the way."

37
00:02:49.237 --> 00:02:53.947
Eurylocus pointed in the direction he had come, and Odysseus grabbed his armor and his sword.

38
00:02:53.948 --> 00:02:58.477
Odysseus had not gone far when suddenly Hermes, disguised as a youthful man, approached him.

39
00:02:58.478 --> 00:03:05.914
"Where do you go?" asked the youth. "I don't know," said Odysseus, "but something terrible has happened to my men."

40
00:03:05.915 --> 00:03:10.008
"Men? Ha, I think not," said the youth, laughing.

41
00:03:10.009 --> 00:03:14.770
"If you mean Circe's pigs, nothing terrible will happen to them until they fatten up."

42
00:03:14.771 --> 00:03:20.141
Odysseus eyed the youth more carefully. "You are not who you seem," said Odysseus.

43
00:03:20.142 --> 00:03:24.046
"This is true," said the youth, "but I can help you.

44
00:03:24.047 --> 00:03:30.220
Take this necklace of herb and wear it beneath your shirt. It will protect you from Circe's enchantments."

45
00:03:30.221 --> 00:03:35.339
Odysseus took the necklace of herbs, and the youth skipped into the sky and was gone.

46
00:03:35.340 --> 00:03:39.943
When Odysseus reached Circe's house, he called out to her, and she invited him in.

47
00:03:39.944 --> 00:03:46.721
"Please sit at my table, and have dinner with me, because I rarely have guests upon my island," she said, smiling wickedly.

48
00:03:46.722 --> 00:03:50.014
Odysseus ate with her, but her spell had no effect.

49
00:03:50.015 --> 00:03:55.999
When she flicked her wand and said, "Off to the pigsty with you!" Odysseus drew his sword and charged her.

50
00:03:56.000 --> 00:04:01.085
Before he could strike her, she fell to her knees, "Who are you and from where have you come?"

51
00:04:01.086 --> 00:04:06.077
"I am Odysseus. If you value your life, you will release my men from your enchantment."

52
00:04:06.078 --> 00:04:09.546
"Put back your sword, mighty hero," said Circe.

53
00:04:09.547 --> 00:04:15.473
"First, you cannot kill me; second, your visit was foretold; and third, we are to become friends."

54
00:04:15.474 --> 00:04:22.782
Circe reached up and gently helped Odysseus to put back his sword, and then she took him by the hand and took him to her room.

55
00:04:22.783 --> 00:04:25.880
Circe found Odysseus sitting the next morning, eating nothing.

56
00:04:25.881 --> 00:04:29.919
"Why do you sulk, noble Odysseus? I promised to do you no harm!"

57
00:04:29.920 --> 00:04:34.641
"You promised to do me no harm, but my men are still pigs squealing and grunting in your sty.

58
00:04:34.642 --> 00:04:37.395
How can I be happy, when my men are thus condemned?"

59
00:04:37.396 --> 00:04:45.115
"Do your men mean that much to you?" asked Circe. Odysseus nodded. "So be it! I will release them."

60
00:04:45.116 --> 00:04:54.822
With a quick flick of her golden wand, the squealing and grunting in the pigsty ceased, and soon the gate to the sty burst open and there stood twenty-two dazed and mud-covered men.

61
00:04:54.823 --> 00:05:00.272
"They are rather unsightly," said Circe. "I simply won't have them in my house until they have bathed."

62
00:05:00.273 --> 00:05:04.232
Circe clapped five times, and five fairy-like nymphs appeared.

63
00:05:04.233 --> 00:05:08.455
"Wash and oil them," she commanded, and the nymphs did as they were ordered.

64
00:05:08.456 --> 00:05:12.197
Odysseus and his men spent many days enjoying Circe's hospitality.

65
00:05:12.198 --> 00:05:18.983
As the days turned into weeks, and the weeks turned into months, Odysseus finally told Circe of his desire to leave.

66
00:05:18.984 --> 00:05:23.291
"You have been very kind, but your kindness is just another kind of enchantment.

67
00:05:23.292 --> 00:05:25.243
It is time for us to leave."

68
00:05:25.244 --> 00:05:33.775
"It is foretold," said Circe. "You will leave, but your journey is not over. Before you can return home, you must seek the prophet named Tiresias."

69
00:05:33.776 --> 00:05:40.235
"And where can I find this prophet?" asked Odysseus. "In the land of the dead," said Circe confidently.

70
00:05:40.236 --> 00:05:45.134
"And how, except by death, do I journey to this land?" asked Odysseus, dismayed.

71
00:05:45.135 --> 00:05:49.139
"Set your whitest sail, smear ash upon your face, and wait.

72
00:05:49.140 --> 00:05:54.296
The wind of death will take you where you need to go." Odysseus bowed his head in resignation.

73
00:05:54.297 --> 00:05:58.722
Unlike mortal men, Circe could read the script that fate had written.

